 them","p_rec":"If Cincinnati's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Cincinnati has set <strong>32 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 63 that chance alone would give, and <strong>66 record highs</strong>, close to the 63 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (65). Cincinnati's all-time high is 108\u00b0F, on July 21, 1934.","t_chg":"Cincinnati's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 153 complete years: Cincinnati's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.14\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.20 to \u22120.08), about \u22122.2\u00b0F since 1873. Cincinnati's average high shows no clear change: +0.05\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.10) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.70\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.35 to +1.05) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.46\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.06 to +0.85). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 7 months fell. Highs rose in 3 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Cincinnati has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Cincinnati's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.55 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.30 to +0.80), about +8.5 inches since 1871. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 45.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 2011 (73.28 in); the driest, 1901 (17.99 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}